BLOCK FB FB_HopperBlockageMonitor
ROOTID 0
NUMBER 50
LANGUAGE LAD
TITLE "Hopper-Blockage Monitor"
COMMENT "Supervises the shredder hopper high-level sensor: raises a latched hopper-blockage alarm (and a stop/inhibit demand) when the hopper stays (debounced-)high for a cumulative threshold of time while the plant is running, i.e. the shredder/pusher is not clearing it (S6 request #1, REQ-HBA-001..007). Purely supervisory - it commands no motion and writes no shared tag; the alarm and the stop demand are exposed on its own IO interface and wired to annunciation / the actual stop at a separate integration step (Q-HBA-05). Cumulative timing is pause-and-resume (NEW-HBA-01): losing PlantRunning freezes the elapsed and resumes, so 60 s of blockage need not be contiguous. Built from a plain TON, never TONR (C-406), plus a banked up-accumulator compared against the threshold (see NETWORK 3-4). The alarm latch lives in the RETAIN interface and deliberately survives a power cycle (C-124 carve-out, NEW-HBA-04) - like the sequencer's DischargeConveyorTimeoutFault, a blockage needing a human still needs FaultReset after a restart; all transient timing state is non-retentive and self-clears at startup (NEW-HBA-06, no OB100 edit). Tunables BlockedTimeThreshold (T#60S) and ClearDebounceTime (T#2S) are this instance's own settings (C-307), not shared DB members."

INTERFACE
  INPUT
  OUTPUT
  STATIC
    IO : "UDT_HopperBlockageIO" RETAIN SETPOINT COMMENT "Caller interface (C-115). Inputs (HopperLevelHigh/PlantRunning/FaultReset) are written by FC_ControlMain at integration; outputs (HopperBlockedAlarm/HopperBlockStopReq) are read there. The whole interface is RETAIN - immaterial for the re-driven inputs, deliberate for the two output latches (C-124 carve-out)."
      HopperLevelHigh : Bool
      PlantRunning : Bool
      FaultReset : Bool
      BlockedTimeThreshold : Time
      ClearDebounceTime : Time
      HopperBlockedAlarm : Bool
      HopperBlockStopReq : Bool
    HighQualified : Bool COMMENT "Debounced blockage-present latch: set the instant the hopper reads high, held through momentary sensor clears (chatter), cleared only once the hopper has read clear continuously for ClearDebounceTime (Q-HBA-02). Transient (non-retentive)."
    BlockActive : Bool COMMENT "The persistence window accrues only while this is true: a qualified blockage AND the plant actually running (Q-HBA-04)."
    BlockActiveMem : Bool COMMENT "Previous-scan BlockActive, for the constructed falling-edge (C-404)."
    PauseEdge : Bool COMMENT "One-scan pulse when BlockActive falls (plant stop or genuine clear) - fires the T_SUB bank in NETWORK 4."
    AccumulatedElapsed : Time COMMENT "Cumulative high-and-running elapsed banked across plant-stop pauses (NEW-HBA-01). Two disjoint writers (NETWORK 3): reset to T#0S on a confirmed clear or FaultReset, banked up by each just-ended segment's elapsed on a plant-stop pause - never both in one scan. Transient (non-retentive); starts 0, so no first-scan trip. Replaces the former countdown RemainingTime (F1/F2/S1 fix, 2026-07-20)."
    CumulativeElapsed : Time COMMENT "Live total = AccumulatedElapsed + PersistenceTimer.ET (NETWORK 4), compared against BlockedTimeThreshold to trip the alarm. Single-writer, derived each scan."
    ZeroTime : Time COMMENT "Constant zero (T#0S) source for the accumulator reset - TIA import rejects a bare Time literal as a MOVE operand, so the zero is held as an unwritten Time member (defaults to T#0S, never written). Non-retentive."
    ClearDebounceTimer : TON_TIME VERSION 1.0 SETPOINT
      PT : Time
      ET : Time
      IN : Bool
      Q : Bool
    PersistenceTimer : TON_TIME VERSION 1.0 SETPOINT
      PT : Time
      ET : Time
      IN : Bool
      Q : Bool

NETWORK 1 "Confirmed-Clear Debounce"
  COMMENT "Times the hopper reading NOT high. Its Q (hopper clear continuously for ClearDebounceTime) is the only thing that re-arms the blockage qualifier - a shorter blip is ignored (Q-HBA-02, debounced clear)."
  TON(ClearDebounceTimer, IN := NOT IO.HopperLevelHigh, PT := IO.ClearDebounceTime)

NETWORK 2 "Blockage-Present Qualifier And Run Gate"
  COMMENT "HighQualified goes true when the hopper reads high and holds through momentary clears, dropping only on a confirmed (debounced) clear - so a chattering sensor cannot re-arm the window (REQ-HBA-003, Q-HBA-02). BlockActive additionally requires the plant to be running (Q-HBA-04)."
  COIL HighQualified := IO.HopperLevelHigh OR HighQualified AND NOT ClearDebounceTimer.Q
  COIL BlockActive := HighQualified AND IO.PlantRunning

NETWORK 3 "Cumulative Blockage-Elapsed Accumulator"
  COMMENT "Banks cumulative high-and-running time across plant-stop pauses (NEW-HBA-01), replacing the former countdown budget (F1/F2/S1 fix, 2026-07-20). AccumulatedElapsed has two provably-disjoint writers, so there is no same-scan race and no next-scan cleanup (the F2/S1 root cause is gone): it is reset to T#0S on a genuine confirmed clear (NOT HighQualified) or on FaultReset, and otherwise banked up by the just-ended segment's elapsed only on a real plant-stop pause (PauseEdge AND HighQualified AND NOT FaultReset - i.e. the hopper is still blocked but the plant stopped). PauseEdge is a constructed falling edge (C-404); it and the bank read PersistenceTimer.ET from before NETWORK 4's TON re-evaluates this scan, so the ET captured is the just-completed segment's, with no separate held-value register needed."
  COIL PauseEdge := NOT BlockActive AND BlockActiveMem
  COIL BlockActiveMem := BlockActive
  MOVE(EN := NOT HighQualified OR IO.FaultReset, IN := ZeroTime) => AccumulatedElapsed
  ADD(EN := PauseEdge AND HighQualified AND NOT IO.FaultReset, IN1 := PersistenceTimer.ET, IN2 := AccumulatedElapsed) => AccumulatedElapsed

NETWORK 4 "Cumulative Persistence Timer (TON, C-406)"
  COMMENT "Plain TON, never TONR (C-406), times the current contiguous high-and-running segment; FaultReset in its IN forces the current segment back to zero so a reset clears the in-progress time too (F1: reset is history-independent). CumulativeElapsed = AccumulatedElapsed (banked pauses) + this segment's live ET is the total cumulative high-and-running time, compared against BlockedTimeThreshold in NETWORK 5. PT is BlockedTimeThreshold only so a single contiguous segment's ET can itself reach the threshold; the trip is the comparison, not the timer's own Q."
  TON(PersistenceTimer, IN := BlockActive AND NOT IO.FaultReset, PT := IO.BlockedTimeThreshold)
  ADD(EN := TRUE, IN1 := AccumulatedElapsed, IN2 := PersistenceTimer.ET) => CumulativeElapsed

NETWORK 5 "Hopper-Blocked Alarm Latch (RETAIN - Survives Power Cycle)"
  COMMENT "Latched fault (C-123): set when cumulative high-and-running time reaches BlockedTimeThreshold (CumulativeElapsed >= threshold), held until FaultReset. FaultReset gates the whole rung (AND NOT FaultReset) so a reset clears the latch regardless of the trip's current state (F1 fix - the old rung let a still-true trip defeat the reset); paired with NETWORK 3/4 zeroing the accumulator and current segment, a reset requires a fresh full accumulation before re-alarming, identically whether the trip was contiguous or post-pause. Lives in the RETAIN interface and deliberately survives a power cycle (C-124 carve-out, NEW-HBA-04) - a blockage needing a human still needs acknowledgement after a restart, like the sequencer's DischargeConveyorTimeoutFault. Fail-safe: the stop demand below stays asserted until acknowledged."
  COIL IO.HopperBlockedAlarm := (CumulativeElapsed >= IO.BlockedTimeThreshold OR IO.HopperBlockedAlarm) AND NOT IO.FaultReset

NETWORK 6 "Stop / Inhibit Demand"
  COMMENT "The FB emits a stop/inhibit demand on its own interface (Q-HBA-05) and writes no shared output - integration wires this demand to the actual stop and the alarm to annunciation at a separate step (out of gen-block-new scope, converter-diff-enforced). Kept a distinct member from the alarm so the two can be routed independently."
  COIL IO.HopperBlockStopReq := IO.HopperBlockedAlarm
